      "latency_ms": 2366.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 635,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water at room temperature, which flows in rivers, fills oceans, and falls as rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the form of an invisible gas, found in the air as humidity and in clouds.\n\nWater is also known as the **\"universal solvent\"** because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This ability is what allows it to transport vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals in living organisms and ecosystems.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n*   Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**.\n*   Most of that freshwater is trapped in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground.\n*   Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n\nWater is constantly moving through a closed system called the **water cycle** (or hydrological cycle). It evaporates from the Earth's surface, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, condenses, and falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain, snow, sleet), where it flows into bodies of water and repeats the process.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n*   **Biological makeup:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water, and the brain is about 73% water. \n*   **Cellular function:** Water provides the medium in which all the metabolic and cellular processes of life take place.\n*   **Regulation:** Water helps regulate body temperature (through sweating and respiration) and the Earth's climate (ocean currents distribute heat around the globe).\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It shapes the geography of our planet, drives our weather and climate, and acts as the literal lifeblood of every living organism."
